    fix(userns): re-arm the parent-death signal after the mapped launch's cred change. Addresses TJ-DF-043.
    
    ROOT CAUSE (named, cited, reproduced on the failing kernel): util-linux
    arms --kill-child's prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG) in the forked child BEFORE the
    -S/-G setgid/setuid, and the kernel clears the parent-death setting on any
    effective uid/gid change (man 2 prctl PR_SET_PDEATHSIG "cleared upon
    changes to any of the following thread credentials: effective user ID,
    effective group ID, filesystem user ID, or filesystem group ID";
    kernel/cred.c commit_creds(): task->pdeath_signal = 0 when euid/egid/
    fsuid/fsgid change). The uid-mapped launch (--map-users ... -S 65534
    -G 65534) performs exactly that change, so its payload ran as namespace
    PID 1 with NO parent-death linkage: namespace-exit reaping does not apply
    to PID 1, and the wrapper's SIGKILL left a real orphan. Measured on
    Debian 13.7 / kernel 6.12.107+deb13-amd64 (bunker-las-03, QA's failing
    host): mapped launch orphan >5s 2/2 reps; LD_PRELOAD syscall-order shim
    showed the real child order prctl(PDEATHSIG) -> setgroups(0) ->
    setgid(65534) -> setuid(65534) -> exec. NOT a kernel-version divergence:
    kernel/cred.c (clear), kernel/exit.c forget_original_parent (delivery),
    kernel/signal.c kill_ok_by_cred (permission) are byte-identical between
    v6.12 and v7.0. The dev host never saw the bug because its AppArmor
    policy denies setuid/setgroups inside unprivileged user namespaces — the
    mapped launch cannot be created there at all, so every green dev-host
    teardown run exercised only the mapping-less shape (whose payload keeps
    the caller's kuid and passes kill_ok_by_cred by uid equality).
    
    FIX (both launch sites, one shape): the mapped launch flags end with a
    `setpriv --pdeathsig=SIGKILL` exec tail — setpriv becomes the exec target
    between unshare's cred change and the payload, re-arming the signal AFTER
    the change that cleared it. Measured on the failing kernel: the identical
    mapped shape tears down in 20 ms, 3/3 reps (raw cells committed under
    docs/dogfood/tjdf043-kernel-cells/, importable via the documented
    --import path; the pre-fix FAIL cells are retained as the defect's
    record). The tail lives INSIDE the flag string, so the preflight probe
    exercises the exact launch: a host whose setpriv lacks --pdeathsig
    (util-linux < 2.36, below the documented floor) fails the probe and falls
    back mapping-less with the existing loud warning — fail-closed, never
    silently selected. The firewall/other containment layers are untouched
    (verify: the full suite, 1575 passed / 7 skipped standalone, includes the
    escape-wave corpus and the fail-closed contract pins).
    
    CONTRACT PINS (this cannot silently return):
    - plugin/test_tjdf043_pdeathsig_rearm.py: the mapped template must end
      with the re-arm tail; --kill-child stays armed; the mapping-less launch
      is unchanged (its shape never had the defect).
    - plugin/test_interruptor.py launch-contract: the uid-mapped rewrite
      WITHOUT the tail (the defect shape) and with a wrong-signal tail are
      now REJECTED fixtures alongside the broken-shape family.
    - scripts/kernel-matrix-teardown.py: new --gate mode (exit 1 only when an
      orphan is OBSERVED on the running host; an unlaunchable wrapper SKIPs
      honestly and is never counted as a pass), wired as a CI cell in
      .github/workflows/ci.yml; 6 gate-classifier unit tests including a
      verdict-flip mutation control.
    - docs/backend-parity.md: known-limit (e) records mechanism + citations;
      the kernel-matrix table now carries the measured Debian cells with
      per-rep survival times; the RELEASE-TJ-008 re-evaluation states
      blocker 1 (QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13) can lift on this evidence and records
      the tool-vs-platform ownership split (narrowed on-any-host guarantee
      here; strong lifecycle guarantee stays with the bunker platform).
    - README (--parent-death note + Requirements), --help, specs/cli.md
      (launch template + parity table), CHANGELOG: the narrowed guarantee
      documented at every surface the row names.
    
    Also .gitreins/config.yaml: guards.test_timeout 180->900 (engine default;
    measured standalone runs 511s/515s/848s) and hook_timeout 300->1200 — the
    commit hook grades this tree's full suite (ci.yml is a force-full glob),
    and a hook-timeout FAIL-OPEN early-return is not gate-verified: the
    smaller budget wins, so both rungs move together.
    
    Gates: .venv/bin/python -m pytest -x --tb=short -> 1575 passed / 7
    skipped standalone (two earlier -x runs stopped on install-test 60/180s
    subprocess budgets under fleet load 25-50; both tests pass in isolation,
    16s/95s). gitreins guard lint+secrets green; tests lane green standalone
    and via the raised budgets (see config comment).
    
